1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,536
How does one system talk to card networks that each speak a different language?

2
00:00:04,586 --> 00:00:07,008
Abroad, you carry a plug adapter.

3
00:00:07,058 --> 00:00:11,424
Every country's socket is different, but your laptop's plug never changes.

4
00:00:11,474 --> 00:00:17,544
Each card processing centre has its own protocol, its own error codes and its own way of saying yes.

5
00:00:17,594 --> 00:00:25,584
So the card zone has one adapter per centre: a small service that speaks the centre's language on one side, and one plain language on the other.

6
00:00:25,634 --> 00:00:29,688
Inside the card zone there is one plug shape for every centre.

7
00:00:29,738 --> 00:00:34,992
Charge, refund, check status: the same questions, whichever centre answers.

8
00:00:35,042 --> 00:00:40,488
And every answer becomes one of three outcomes: approved, declined, or unknown.

9
00:00:40,538 --> 00:00:45,672
A timeout is unknown, never declined, because the money may already have moved.

10
00:00:45,722 --> 00:00:51,936
So every charge carries our own payment id, and later we can ask the centre exactly what happened to it.

11
00:00:51,986 --> 00:00:55,536
A new centre means a new adapter, and nothing else changes.

12
00:00:55,586 --> 00:00:58,992
Next: how the card zone keeps a card number safe.
